    primarySourceText: "IDA B. WELLS BEGINS ANTI-LYNCHING CAMPAIGN (1892)\n══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\nLocation: Memphis, Tennessee → Chicago, Illinois, US\nEra: Era 4: Colonial Conquest & Resistance\nCategory: Intellectual / political organizing\nSource Tier: Tier 2: Primary Documents\n\nOn March 9, 1892, three of Ida B. Wells's friends — Thomas Moss, Calvin McDowell, and Will Stewart — were lynched in Memphis. Their crime: the People's Grocery Company, which they co-owned, had competed successfully with a white-owned grocery, and a white mob attacked them. Wells, a 29-year-old journalist and co-owner of the *Free Speech and Headlight* newspaper, launched an editorial campaign calling lynching what it was: terrorism designed to suppress Black economic independence, not a response to Black crime (specifically, not a response to the rape myth). She wrote: \"Nobody in this section of the country believes the old threadbare lie that Negro men rape white women.\" Her offices were destroyed by a white mob; she fled Memphis and never returned. She published *Southern Horrors* (1892) and *A Red Record* (1895) — the first systematic statistical documentation of lynching. She toured the UK in 1893 and 1894, building international pressure on the US. She is the founder of investigative journalism on racial terror, and she did it as a Black woman in the 1890s.\n\nPAN-AFRICAN BRIDGE:\nWells's UK tour (1893, 1894) is the direct precursor to the 1900 Pan-African Conference in London — she and Sylvester Williams were building the same transatlantic network. → `pan-african-conference-1900`\n\nTHREADS:\n- press-radio-counter-media\n- women-in-liberation\n- the-body-as-battleground\n\nSOURCE CITATIONS:\n- 2 (Wells's pamphlets and *A Red Record*) + 3 (Paula Giddings, *Ida: A Sword Among Lions*)"
