    primarySourceText: "PAN-AFRICAN CONFERENCE (1900)\n══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\nLocation: London, UK\nEra: Era 5: Pan-African Crystallization\nCategory: Political organizing\nSource Tier: Tier 2: Primary Documents\n\nOrganized by Trinidadian lawyer Henry Sylvester Williams, the Pan-African Conference met July 23–25, 1900 at Westminster Town Hall. Thirty-two delegates attended — from Africa (Ethiopia, Sierra Leone, Liberia, Gold Coast), the Caribbean (Jamaica, Trinidad, Haiti), the US, and the UK. W.E.B. Du Bois attended and drafted the \"Address to the Nations of the World,\" which contained the line: \"The problem of the twentieth century is the problem of the colour-line.\" The Conference demanded self-government for African and Caribbean colonies, the protection of Indigenous rights in South Africa and the Americas, and an end to racial discrimination. The Conference established the Pan-African Association, which dissolved within a year, but the precedent was set: subsequent Pan-African Congresses followed in 1919 (Paris), 1921 (London/Brussels/Paris), 1923 (London/Lisbon), 1927 (New York), and 1945 (Manchester). The 1945 Congress — where Nkrumah and Kenyatta were present — directly led to the independence movements of the 1950s and 60s.\n\nPAN-AFRICAN BRIDGE:\nThis is the formal founding moment of organized Pan-Africanism — 18 years before Garvey's UNIA, 57 years before Ghana's independence. Every subsequent Pan-African institution traces back to Westminster Town Hall, July 1900. → `garvey-unia-1920` · `ghana-independence-1957`\n\nTHREADS:\n- the-word-as-weapon\n- solidarity-beyond-africa\n\nSOURCE CITATIONS:\n- 2 (Conference proceedings) + 3 (Hakim Adi, *Pan-Africanism: A History*)"
