    primarySourceText: "*THE SOULS OF BLACK FOLK* PUBLISHED (1903)\n══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\nLocation: Atlanta, Georgia, US\nEra: Era 5: Pan-African Crystallization\nCategory: Intellectual / literary\nSource Tier: Tier 2: Primary Documents\n\nW.E.B. Du Bois's *The Souls of Black Folk* was published April 18, 1903. The book collects 14 essays that map the spiritual, psychological, and political landscape of Black America at the turn of the century. It introduces the concept of \"double-consciousness\" — the experience of always seeing oneself through the eyes of a racist society: \"It is a peculiar sensation, this double-consciousness, this sense of always looking at one's self through the eyes of others.\" It argues that the \"Talented Tenth\" of Black America must lead. It contains the line that defined the century: \"The problem of the Twentieth Century is the problem of the color-line.\" It pairs scholarly essays with personal narrative (the death of his infant son) and political analysis (Booker T. Washington's accommodationism). It is the foundational text of modern African-American intellectual life.\n\nPAN-AFRICAN BRIDGE:\nDu Bois would go on to attend every Pan-African Congress from 1919 to 1945, eventually renounce his US citizenship, and move to Ghana at Nkrumah's invitation in 1961 to work on the *Encyclopedia Africana*. His arc spans the entire Pan-African crystallization. → `ghana-independence-1957` · `pan-african-conference-1900`\n\nTHREADS:\n- the-word-as-weapon\n- education-for-liberation\n\nSOURCE CITATIONS:\n- 2 (Du Bois primary text) + 3 (David Levering Lewis, *W.E.B. Du Bois: Biography of a Race*)"
