    title: "NAACP Founded",
    summary: "The National Association for the Advancement of Colored People was founded February 12, 1909 — the centennial of Abraham Lincoln's birth — by a multiracial group of activists including W.E.B. Du Bois, Ida B. Wells, Archibald Grimké, Mary White Ovington, Moorfield Storey, and Oswald Garrison Villard. The precipitating event was the 1908 Springfield race riot in Lincoln's hometown. The NAACP's primary weapon became the lawsuit. They built a legal strategy — first against restrictive covenants, then against school segregation, then against lynching — that culminated in *Brown v. Board of Education* (1954) and *Loving v. Virginia* (1967). Their magazine *The Crisis*, edited by Du Bois from 1910 to 1934, became the most important Black intellectual journal in the US and published Langston Hughes, Countee Cullen, and other Harlem Renaissance writers. Thurgood Marshall, who would argue *Brown* and become the first Black Supreme Court Justice, was the NAACP's chief counsel from 1940 to 1961.",
