    sourceTier: "Tier 3: African Scholars",
    region: "Americas",
    title: "*The Birth of a Nation* Released",
    summary: "D.W. Griffith's *The Birth of a Nation* premiered February 8, 1915, in Los Angeles. The three-hour film depicted the Civil War and Reconstruction as a tragedy of Black misrule and glorified the Ku Klux Klan as the savior of white Southern civilization. It was the first film screened at the White House — by Woodrow Wilson, a Southerner whose own published history had been used as source material for the film. Wilson reportedly said: \"It is like writing history with lightning. And my only regret is that it is all so terribly true.\" The film revived the Klan, which had been dormant since the 1870s; membership reached 4 million by 1925. The NAACP, founded just six years earlier, protested and tried to have the film banned — they failed, but they built a national organization in the attempt. The film is a foundational example of popular culture doing the work of white supremacy.",
