    primarySourceText: "*THE BIRTH OF A NATION* RELEASED (1915)\n══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\nLocation: Los Angeles, US\nEra: Era 5: Pan-African Crystallization\nCategory: Film / state violence\nSource Tier: Tier 3: African Scholars\n\nD.W. Griffith's *The Birth of a Nation* premiered February 8, 1915, in Los Angeles. The three-hour film depicted the Civil War and Reconstruction as a tragedy of Black misrule and glorified the Ku Klux Klan as the savior of white Southern civilization. It was the first film screened at the White House — by Woodrow Wilson, a Southerner whose own published history had been used as source material for the film. Wilson reportedly said: \"It is like writing history with lightning. And my only regret is that it is all so terribly true.\" The film revived the Klan, which had been dormant since the 1870s; membership reached 4 million by 1925. The NAACP, founded just six years earlier, protested and tried to have the film banned — they failed, but they built a national organization in the attempt. The film is a foundational example of popular culture doing the work of white supremacy.\n\nPAN-AFRICAN BRIDGE:\n*The Birth of a Nation* is the most consequential piece of white-supremacist propaganda in modern history. Its counterpart — a popular cultural work that re-centered Black dignity — would not arrive on the same scale until *Black Panther* (2018), 103 years later. → `black-panther-film-2018`\n\nTHREADS:\n- music-as-memory\n- press-radio-counter-media\n\nSOURCE CITATIONS:\n- 3 (Melvyn Stokes, *D.W. Griffith's The Birth of a Nation*) + 4 (NAACP protest records)"
