    primarySourceText: "RED SUMMER + CLAUDE MCKAY'S \"IF WE MUST DIE\" (1919)\n══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\nLocation: Multiple US cities\nEra: Era 5: Pan-African Crystallization\nCategory: State violence / literary\nSource Tier: Tier 2: Primary Documents\n\nThe \"Red Summer\" of 1919 saw racial violence in 26 US cities from May through October — Chicago (38 dead), Washington DC, Elaine Arkansas (estimates 100–237 dead, mostly Black sharecroppers attempting to unionize), Knoxville, Omaha, and others. White mobs attacked Black communities; in Chicago, 38 died and 537 were injured over five days. In Elaine, the federal government deployed troops — who joined the white mobs. The violence was triggered by post-WWI competition for jobs and housing, and by white rage at returning Black veterans who expected the democracy they had fought for. Claude McKay, a Jamaican poet living in Harlem, responded with the sonnet \"If We Must Die\" — published in the July 1919 *Liberator* magazine: \"If we must die, let it not be like hogs / Hunted and penned in an inglorious spot... / Like men we'll face the murderous, cowardly pack, / Pressed to the wall, dying, but fighting back!\" Winston Churchill later quoted it in a 1940 address to the US Congress. The Red Summer catalyzed the \"New Negro\" militancy that defined the Harlem Renaissance.\n\nPAN-AFRICAN BRIDGE:\nMcKay — Jamaican-born — is the literal embodiment of the Pan-African web. He would travel to London in 1920, to the Soviet Union in 1922, and to Morocco and France in the 1930s. His 1928 novel *Home to Harlem* is the diaspora novel. → `garvey-unia-1920` · `new-negro-1925`\n\nTHREADS:\n- the-word-as-weapon\n- the-body-as-battleground\n\nSOURCE CITATIONS:\n- 2 (McKay poem) + 3 (Cameron McWhirter, *Red Summer*)"
