    primarySourceText: "*THE NEW NEGRO* ANTHOLOGY (1925)\n══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\nLocation: New York, NY, US\nEra: Era 5: Pan-African Crystallization\nCategory: Intellectual / literary\nSource Tier: Tier 2: Primary Documents\n\nEdited by Alain Locke — the first Black Rhodes Scholar and a Howard University philosophy professor — *The New Negro: An Interpretation* was published November 1925. The anthology collected the leading voices of the Harlem Renaissance: Langston Hughes, Zora Neale Hurston, Countee Cullen, Claude McKay, Jean Toomer, Jessie Fauset, and others. Locke's introductory essay declared: \"The day of 'aunties,' 'uncles' and 'mammies' is... gone.\" The Old Negro of submission and apology was dead; the New Negro of self-determination, artistic pride, and political militancy was born. The anthology's publication marked the formal institutional recognition of the Harlem Renaissance as a movement. It also argued that Black art was the primary means of Black liberation — a position that would be contested (by, among others, Richard Wright and Ralph Ellison) but that defined the era's self-image.\n\nPAN-AFRICAN BRIDGE:\nThe New Negro ideology is the diaspora counterpart to the négritude movement forming in Paris in the same years (Senghor, Césaire, Damas). Both movements argued that cultural self-affirmation was the precondition for political liberation. → `negritude-paris-1934`\n\nTHREADS:\n- the-word-as-weapon\n- education-for-liberation\n\nSOURCE CITATIONS:\n- 2 (Locke anthology) + 3 (Henry Louis Gates, *Figures in Black*)"
