    title: "March on Washington + 16th Street Baptist Church Bombing",
    summary: "April 16, 1963: From his Birmingham jail cell, MLK wrote the \"Letter from Birmingham Jail\" — answering white moderate clergymen who said the movement was moving too fast. \"Injustice anywhere is a threat to justice everywhere... I have almost reached the regrettable conclusion that the Negro's great stumbling block in his stride toward freedom is not the White Citizen's Counciler or the Ku Klux Klanner, but the white moderate.\" August 28, 1963: 250,000 people gathered at the Lincoln Memorial for the March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom — organized by A. Philip Randolph (who had threatened a similar march in 1941 and forced FDR to desegregate defense industries) and Bayard Rustin (the chief logistical organizer, who was marginalized because he was gay). King's \"I Have a Dream\" speech was the headline, but the march's full title — \"for Jobs and Freedom\" — names the economic justice agenda largely forgotten. September 15, 1963: The Klan bombed the 16th Street Baptist Church in Birmingham, killing four girls — Addie Mae Collins (14), Cynthia Wesley (14), Carole Robertson (14), Denise McNair (11). The bombing horrified the world and accelerated the Civil Rights Act of 1964.",
