    region: "Global",
    title: "Notting Hill Carnival Riot",
    summary: "On August 30, 1976 (the last day of the August bank holiday carnival), police attempted to shut down the Notting Hill Carnival — Europe's largest street festival and the cultural centerpiece of Black British life. They seized sound systems, deployed riot police, and tried to disperse crowds. Black youth resisted. The riot lasted the evening; 600 police officers were injured, ~60 carnival-goers were injured, and 60 people were arrested. The 1976 riot was the first of a series of confrontations at the Carnival through the 1980s — 1977, 1980, 1982, 1985. The Carnival — born from Claudia Jones's 1959 indoor Mardi Gras at St Pancras Town Hall (in response to the 1958 Notting Hill race riots and the murder of Kelso Cochrane) — had become too big to police, and the state's attempt to contain it generated the very confrontation it claimed to be preventing. The Carnival has since become institutionalized — police participate rather than suppress — but the 1976 riot remains a foundational moment of Black British resistance to cultural policing.",
    mediaType: "text",
    mediaPayload: "C:/Ppls_Story/notting-hill-carnival-riot.txt",
    primarySourceCitation: "Notting Hill Carnival Riot (London, UK, 1976); Source: 3 (Cecil Gutzmore, \"Carnival, the State and the Black Masses in the UK\") + 4 (Race & Class archive)",
