    primarySourceText: "NEW CROSS FIRE + BRIXTON UPRISING (1981)\n══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\nLocation: London, UK\nEra: Era 7: Post-Independence & Setbacks\nCategory: State violence / political organizing\nSource Tier: Tier 3: African Scholars\n\nJanuary 18, 1981: A fire at 439 New Cross Road, Deptford, killed 13 young Black people at a joint 16th birthday party for Yvonne Ruddock and Angela Jackson. The police treated it as an accident (probably arson by fascists); no one was ever convicted. The Black community believed the police had no interest in solving it — the press coverage was minimal, no government statement was issued for weeks. March 2, 1981: 20,000 Black people marched through London in the Black People's Day of Action — the largest Black demonstration in UK history. The march's headline: \"13 Dead, Nothing Said.\" April 10–12, 1981: Brixton Uprising. Operation \"Swamp 81\" — police flooded Brixton, stopping and searching 943 people in 5 days (mostly young Black men). On April 10, after police attempted to assist a Black youth who had been stabbed and was being helped by community members, the situation escalated. Two days of rebellion: 279 police injured, 45 civilians injured, 118 vehicles burned, 145 buildings damaged, £7.5 million in property damage. The Scarman Report (November 1981) acknowledged \"racial disadvantage\" but rejected institutional racism. Brixton 1981 — with Toxteth (Liverpool), Handsworth (Birmingham), Chapeltown (Leeds), Moss Side (Manchester) uprisings that summer — was the UK's Black Power moment, even though it didn't use that name.\n\nPAN-AFRICAN BRIDGE:\nThe New Cross Fire is the UK Black community's Emmett Till — a moment that crystallized the perception that the state did not value Black life. The Brixton Uprising is the UK counterpart to Watts (1965) and Soweto (1976) — all were moments when police violence triggered sustained collective resistance. → `emmett-till-1955` · `soweto-1976` · `watts-1965`\n\nTHREADS:\n- the-body-as-battleground\n- press-radio-counter-media\n- women-in-liberation\n\nSOURCE CITATIONS:\n- 3 (John Narayan, *United Kingdom?*) + 4 (Scarman Report, Race Today coverage)"
