    title: "MOVE Bombing",
    summary: "MOVE was a Black back-to-nature liberation organization founded by John Africa (Vincent Leaphart) in 1972 in Philadelphia. Members took \"Africa\" as a surname, wore dreadlocks, ate raw food, and refused to cooperate with state institutions. On August 8, 1978, after a year-long police standoff, police attempted to evict MOVE from their Powelton Village house. Officer James Ramp was killed by friendly fire; nine MOVE members were convicted of his murder and sentenced to 30–100 years. MOVE relocated to 6221 Osage Avenue in Cobbs Creek, a Black working-class neighborhood. Neighbors complained about MOVE's sanitation practices and bullhorn announcements. On May 13, 1985, Philadelphia police — under Mayor Wilson Goode, the city's first Black mayor — attempted to evict MOVE. After a day-long gun battle, police dropped a C-4 explosive (supplied by the FBI) from a state helicopter onto the MOVE house roof. The bombing killed 11 MOVE members (including 5 children — among them Africa's only child, Birdie Africa, who survived), destroyed 61 homes, and left 250 people homeless. The only such aerial bombing of a US civilian residence by US police in history. No city official was ever charged. The survivors' children were eventually awarded $1 million each in damages; the city demolished the rebuilt homes in 2000. The MOVE bombing is the most extreme single act of state violence against a Black organization in US history.",
    mediaType: "text",
    mediaPayload: "C:/Ppls_Story/move-bombing.txt",
    primarySourceCitation: "MOVE Bombing (Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, US, 1985); Source: 3 (Robin Wagner-Pacifici, *The Art of Surrender*) + 4 (MOVE Organization writings, Mumia Abu-Jamal coverage)",
