    primarySourceText: "CHARLESTON MASSACRE + BREE NEWSOME REMOVES CONFEDERATE FLAG (2015)\n══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\nLocation: Charleston, South Carolina, US\nEra: Era 8: Contemporary\nCategory: State violence / political organizing\nSource Tier: Tier 3: African Scholars\n\nJune 17, 2015: Dylann Roof, a 21-year-old white supremacist, attended a Wednesday night Bible study at Emanuel African Methodist Episcopal Church in Charleston — one of the oldest Black congregations in the South (founded 1816, the church of Denmark Vesey). After an hour of study, Roof stood up, said \"I have to do it. You rape our women and you're taking over our country. And you have to go,\" and opened fire. He murdered nine Black churchgoers: Clementa Pinckney (the church's pastor and a state senator), Cynthia Hurd, Susie Jackson, Ethel Lance, Depayne Middleton-Doctor, Tywanza Sanders, Daniel Simmons, Sharonda Singleton, Myra Thompson. June 27, 2015 (10 days later): Bree Newsome, a 30-year-old Black activist and filmmaker, climbed the 30-foot flagpole at the South Carolina statehouse in Columbia and removed the Confederate battle flag — the same flag Roof had posed with in his manifesto photos. She was arrested; the flag was raised again within an hour. But the action was captured on video and went viral. July 10, 2015: The South Carolina legislature voted to permanently remove the flag. The Charleston massacre was a reminder that white supremacist violence had never gone away; the flag removal was a symbolic, partial reckoning.\n\nPAN-AFRICAN BRIDGE:\nEmanuel AME's history connects directly to Denmark Vesey's 1822 planned slave revolt — the church was burned in retaliation, and the congregation rebuilt. The Charleston massacre is the modern echo of the 1963 16th Street Baptist Church bombing — both were white supremacist attacks on Black churches, both targeted prayer spaces, both killed Black worshippers. → `birmingham-1963`\n\nTHREADS:\n- the-body-as-battleground\n- faith-as-foundation\n- women-in-liberation\n\nSOURCE CITATIONS:\n- 3 (Jennifer Berry Hawes, *Grace Will Lead Us Home*) + 4 (Emanuel Church archives)"
