    summary: "August 14, 2016: San Francisco 49ers quarterback Colin Kaepernick (28) sat on the bench during the national anthem before a preseason game against the Houston Texans. He told NFL Media afterward: \"I am not going to stand up to show pride in a flag for a country that oppresses Black people and people of color.\" August 26, 2016: After consultation with former Green Beret and NFL long snapper Nate Boyer, Kaepernick switched to kneeling — a posture Boyer suggested as more respectful to military while still a clear protest. Kaepernick was joined by teammate Eric Reid, then by players across the NFL, then by athletes across sports and countries. Kaepernick opted out of his 49ers contract in March 2017 (expecting to sign elsewhere); every NFL team refused to sign him. He filed a collusion grievance against the NFL in October 2017; the case was settled in February 2019 for an undisclosed sum (reported $60–80 million split among Kaepernick, Reid, and lawyers). The protest — and the backlash — became a cultural flashpoint. Kaepernick's kneeling posture was reproduced globally: English Premier League players took the knee from June 2020 (after George Floyd) through 2023; protesters in Hong Kong adopted the gesture; US Women's National Soccer Team players knelt before matches. Kaepernick is the most influential single athlete protest since 1968.",
