    primarySourceText: "JUNETEENTH BECOMES FEDERAL HOLIDAY (2021)\n══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\nLocation: Washington DC, US\nEra: Era 8: Contemporary\nCategory: Political organizing / community\nSource Tier: Tier 2: Primary Documents\n\nJune 17, 2021: President Joe Biden signed the Juneteenth National Independence Day Act, making June 19 a US federal holiday. June 19, 1865: Union General Gordon Granger arrived in Galveston, Texas, and read General Order No. 3: \"The people of Texas are informed that, in accordance with a proclamation from the Executive of the United States, all slaves are free.\" The Emancipation Proclamation had been issued January 1, 1863 — two and a half years earlier. The 250,000 enslaved people in Texas had not been told. Juneteenth (a portmanteau of \"June\" and \"nineteenth\") has been celebrated in Black communities — particularly in Texas and the Black communities that Texas migrants built in Los Angeles, Oakland, Milwaukee, and elsewhere — since 1866. The 2021 federal recognition was the result of decades of organizing — most notably by Opal Lee, a 94-year-old Fort Worth woman who walked from Fort Worth to Washington DC in 2016 to call for the holiday. The recognition was symbolic; the substantive demands of the 2020 uprising (defund the police, end cash bail, reparations, voting rights protection) went largely unmet. The June 17 signing came 11 days after Derek Chauvin's conviction (April 20) and 4 months after the January 6 Capitol insurrection.\n\nPAN-AFRICAN BRIDGE:\nJuneteenth is the most-watched single diaspora commemoration of emancipation. The two-and-a-half-year delay between the Emancipation Proclamation and the Texas announcement is itself a parable of how freedom under white supremacy is always partial, always delayed — the same parable that runs from Lei Áurea (1888) through Ghana independence (1957) through the end of apartheid (1994) to the unresolved demands of 2020. → `lei-aurea-1888` · `ghana-independence-1957` · `mandela-1994`\n\nTHREADS:\n- the-body-as-battleground\n- land-and-sovereignty\n- press-radio-counter-media\n\nSOURCE CITATIONS:\n- 2 (General Order No. 3, Juneteenth legislative text) + 3 (Annette Gordon-Reed, *On Juneteenth*)"
