    excerpt: "James Somerset, an enslaved man brought to England from Boston by Charles Stewart, escaped in October 1771 and was recaptured and chained to a ship bound for Jamaica.",
    commentary: "James Somerset, an enslaved man brought to England from Boston by Charles Stewart, escaped in October 1771 and was recaptured and chained to a ship bound for Jamaica. Abolitionist Granville Sharp filed a writ of habeas corpus. Lord Mansfield, Chief Justice, ruled June 22, 1772 that slavery was \"so odious\" that positive law would be required to sustain it — and no such law existed in England. The ruling did not abolish slavery in the British Empire (that took until 1833), but it effectively freed the ~15,000 enslaved people on English soil and became the legal wedge that the abolitionist movement would expand. Somerset himself disappeared from the historical record after the trial — his job was done.",
    citation: "Somerset v Stewart (London, UK, 1772); Source: 2 (court record) + 3 (Sharp's writings)",
    relatedEventId: "somerset-v-stewart",
    fullTextVfsPath: "C:/Ppls_Story/somerset-v-stewart.txt"
